diff --git a/src/backend/access/heap/README.XID64 b/src/backend/access/heap/README.XID64
index 9b4e6e03a22..41f8859d331 100644
--- a/src/backend/access/heap/README.XID64
+++ b/src/backend/access/heap/README.XID64
@@ -125,10 +125,23 @@ This lazy conversion is called only on pages being read. This can slow down
 performance after upgrade, but just for a short period of time while "hot"
 pages are read (and therefore converted to 64-bit format).
 
-There is a special case when the first read of a tuple is done in read-only
-state (in read-only transaction or on replica). This tuples are to be converted
-"in memory", but not sync "to disk", unless cluster or transaction changed to
-read-write state (e.g. replica is promoted). In order to support this, we mark
-"in memory" pages with converted tuples with bit REGBUF_CONVERTED in buffer
-descriptor. When in read-write state this will trigger full page write xlog
-record.
+Physical input may be asynchronous, but the reading backend validates and
+converts a private page copy under the buffer's exclusive content lock. Normal
+read APIs do not expose the page until conversion has finished. An ordinary
+conversion error before installation leaves the shared legacy page unchanged;
+a later attempt is possible, but malformed data still requires repair.
+
+AIO completion sets BM_VALID together with BM_CONVERSION_PENDING and
+BM_CONVERSION_NEEDS_WAL for a legacy page. BM_VALID denotes physical input;
+the pending flag denotes unfinished conversion, not ownership of the converter.
+The content lock serializes competing converters.
+
+For a permanent relation when WAL insertion is allowed, the backend installs
+the converted copy, marks the buffer dirty, emits a forced XLOG_FPI and sets
+the page LSN before atomically clearing both conversion flags. This applies
+to read-only queries too. Recovery and operations deliberately skipping WAL
+retain a clean converted page with an outstanding WAL obligation. A later
+WAL-logged change must cover that conversion; this includes promotion after
+a standby converted the page in memory. Unlogged pages are dirtied without
+ordinary WAL. These flags are buffer-descriptor state and are never read from
+or written into the on-disk page header.
